Ontology (@ontology: 0, is #chapter, #instinct, #part-of @first-steps, #done) { r_S8PQ17MQ3WBWM53ZN700K89V9F

Ontology is the study of being and existence. When we ask ourselves what data I have in my Lince, what kind of data, how do i manage such data, how do I share data in general, integrate with other Lince/systems, we are talking about Ontology.

We have a Record, it can be given a slug (nickname) as @my-record. Records can have assertions that use a Lingua Vocabulary, it is a way for people to make sure they are talking about the same thing, when you attatch #done to a Record, if in my Vocabulary/Ontology it means the same thing, we can share workflows that have predictable consequences, we can collaborate better. When I make an assertion alone, i am applying a Concept to a Record, 'Eating Lunch' #done, when i assert with another Record, I am making a link: 'Eat Apple' #part-of @nutrition (the slug of the 'Nutrition' Record).

Your Lince is a Cell, you can make a public facing entity that is called an Organ, and a bunch of devices/Cells you have can be in that Organ. The Organs you participate in can be of your public profile, work, family, friends, hobbies...

One can sync their data to a file, or to other Organs, controlling with Protein (used in the interface to get data too), filtering what is sent of Records/Karma/Concepts and what properties of them, to what Organ.

Ontology has these parts and many more. They manage how people interact, the meanings and connections. The other parts of Lince are focused on specific workflows, of Transfers between people, automations with Karma and interactions of Interface.

} r_S8PQ17MQ3WBWM53ZN700K89V9F

Assertion (@assertion: 0, #instinct, #part-of @ontology) {
Records can be given ideas they represent:

Subject --> Predicate
Apple       #apple

That is called applying a concept to a Record.

When you make an assertion that a Record is something in relation to another Record, you are making a link:

Subject --> Predicate --> Object
Hand        #part-of      Arm

An assertion can have the place of the Identity of a Record, being the concept that represents that apple most. When you want to understand what something is that has a bunch of assertions, you look for the main one, the Identity:

Apple is #apple
}


Organ (@organ: 0, #instinct, #part-of @ontology, #done) { r_VAX3HA21RXF9AENHMCJTSEF1AT


Every Record so far lived alone on one machine. This is about what happens when
people show up: your own Cell, other people’s Cells, and the Organs that let
several Cells hold something in common.

## Organ: the sovereign Record holder

A **Cell** is one running Lince instance and its local data. An **Organ** is
the social/network boundary a Cell represents (personal, family, company,
project, community); other reachable Lince nodes are Organs regardless of
their internal arrangement. An Organ is a `kind = organ` Record; it owns its
Records, structure, policy, credentials, and private Lingua. Every Record
carries its origin Organ, preserved through relaying.

All of the following is built; §11 holds the mechanics.

Contacts live in `organ_contact`: `trust` (unknown/known/blocked), numeric
`proximity` (local-only, never exported), and independent `sync_out`/`sync_in`
policy. `blocked` is terminal everywhere — import, discovery, export and
delivery all reject. Introduction exchanges identity + public keys, and
adopting stores the remote Organ under its own uid; Organ identity is
transport/trust, not permission for one Person to act or sign for another.
Sync carries visibility-gated op batches through a durable outbox with retry;
imports verify hash-chain + signature, rejects go to quarantine verbatim while
valid items still apply, import is idempotent by op identity and uid, and
quantity deltas commute. Protein selects Records by origin Organ
(`organ_eq`/`organ_in`).

File Sync mirrors selected Records to Markdown files through the
`lince.file_sync` extension (`enabled`, `path`); disk edits return through the
normal `EditRecordText` action. Disk wins on a same-tick conflict, and a
missing file deletes its Record only after two consecutive misses — a debounce
against atomic editor saves. A live config supervisor
(`engine::file_sync::spawn_supervisor`) starts and stops watchers when config
toggles, reacting to `SetExtension` facts on the bus rather than requiring a
reboot; boot seeds via the same reconcile pass.

The Organ interface lists `kind = organ` Records, edits File Sync config, and
— folded into the same sand as a friends-list panel — edits contact trust and
proximity, with block/unblock as `trust: "blocked"`/`"known"`
(`set-contact-trust`/`set-contact-proximity` Actions, `contact` Protein
include on `organ_contact`). Its scope is trust/proximity; sync policy
(`sync_out`/`sync_in`) and quarantine inspection stay out of that component.
It has since grown Profile, Devices and Discovery panels — §11.

## The Cell

Everything so far happened in one place: your **Cell**. A Cell is your own Lince — your records, on your machine, answering to you.

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["Cell"]
```

## Cells Have No Main One

Other people have their own. No Cell is inside any other, and none of them is the "main" one:

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["Cell"]
  B["Cell"]
  C["Cell"]
  D["Cell"]
```

## The Organ

When several Cells want to hold something in common — a household budget, a team backlog, a shared shopping list — they form an **Organ**.

```mermaid
graph TD
  O["Organ"]
  A["Cell"] --> O
  B["Cell"] --> O
  C["Cell"] --> O
  D["Cell"] --> O
```

_An Organ is a shared context, not a server that owns the members._

## Organs Do Not Blur Together

You belong to as many Organs as you have contexts, and they do not blur into each other. Work does not see your hobby; your family does not see your work backlog.

```mermaid
graph TD
  B["Work"] --> A["Your Cell"]
  C["Hobby"] --> A
  D["Family"] --> A
  E["Friends"] --> A
```

## Lince Has No Centre

Lince as a whole is just all of it — Cells and Organs, with no centre:

```mermaid
graph TD
  A["Organ"] --> O["Lince"]
  B["Cell"] --> O
  C["Organ"] --> O
  D["Cell"] --> O
```

This is why the frontend keeps **areas** (in the workspace menu) separate from each other, and why a Protein can filter by organ. The boundary is real, and it is the same boundary the next chapter has to cross.

} r_VAX3HA21RXF9AENHMCJTSEF1AT

