Lingua (@lingua: 0, #instinct, #part-of @ontology) {
  A Language for turning Lince's data into file versions with File Sync.

  We call a Vocabulary the bundle of Assertions that come from the same Organ, is if we create some dialect, some units, and they mean something to us. When people use the same Vocabulary it is expected that they are speaking about the same thing. So if a Lingua file is for talking to the file system with sync, a Lingua Vocabulary is for talking with other people with common ground. Like a dictionary, with the meanings you give it. We can have an Organ deemed by us as the official when talking about a subject, we just such and such Vocabulary. Like a standard, ISO.
}

Sync (@sync: 0, #instinct, #part-of @ontology) {



--- Eiai 

Sync and CRDT are one capability: an authenticated connection to another Organ
carrying deltas over one channel. Per contact, the connection has a
persistence mode. **Live**: data stays at the remote Organ; a sand accesses it
in memory over a Protein WebSocket, writes go through the *remote* Organ's
change handler, fanning out to every connected peer — nothing stored locally.
**Replica**: syncable tables are copied locally and kept converged.

Syncable tables: `record`, `record_extension`, `concept`, `record_assertion`,
`record_fact`. `organ_contact` (trust/proximity) never leaves a Cell.

The unit of sync is the **op**, not the row: every local write becomes a
field-level operation `(table, uid, field, value, hlc, actor_cell,
organ_uid)`, appended to a local **op log**. An HLC (hybrid logical clock)
follows wall-clock time but is bumped past any remote timestamp it sees, so
"latest wins" stays consistent and never goes backwards.

Two mechanisms keep replicas converged, neither ever rescans a table:
1. **Reactive deltas** — every write enqueues to every `sync_out` contact in
   the same statement (bounded outbox: one queued op per `(contact, tbl, uid,
   field)`).
2. **Catch-up reconciliation** — a version-vector exchange (`FetchOpsSince`,
   keyed `actor_cell → max hlc`, one entry per device), every 30s and on
   reconnect. Converged means an empty answer.

**Merge** splits along one line: **record text** (`head`/`body`) lives in a
Loro doc per record (character-level CRDT), materialized to SQLite on every
change — queries/Protein/File Sync/Archive read only SQLite. Everything else
(scalars, extension namespaces, assertions, concepts) is homebrew per-field
LWW ops with HLC ordering. Quantity is never a CRDT value — it is an
additive fold of signed facts. Deletes are tombstone ops that replicate like
any write.

**Security posture:** encrypted, authenticated, forward-secret in transit via
iroh's QUIC/TLS 1.3. **Plaintext at rest** on both ends, including thread
bodies — a deliberate decision (2026-08-03), not an oversight. If ever
revived, scope is exactly `replica_root IS NOT NULL`.

---

## What is built

Two Lince instances find each other on a network, talk, exchange keys, become
contacts, and sync exactly what each chooses to share, with live editing over
an internet-reachable Cell. This runs end to end.

### Transport (iroh)

- Two Organs reach each other over iroh: QUIC with an ed25519 node key,
  address resolution by DNS/pkarr/mDNS, hole punching, relay fallback.
- **Node key ≠ identity key.** Node key authenticates a live connection
  (per-Cell, never leaves the device, cheap to rotate). Identity key
  authenticates durable bytes — op batches, facts, roster (per-Organ,
  published). Only the NodeId is shared; the peer's identity key + a
  signature binding it to that NodeId arrive over the connection.
- The old HTTP peer path (`/organ/introduction`, `/organ/inbox`,
  `/organ/ops`, `peer_auth`) is deleted, not superseded. Payload signing
  (op-batch provenance, domain prefix `lince/peer/1\n`) was kept separately —
  transport auth answers "who is on this socket", signing answers "who wrote
  this op".
- Three versioned ALPNs, now epoch `/2`: `lince/sync/1` (known contacts),
  `lince/thread/1` (strangers — Introduction, invites, enrolment),
  `lince/live/1` (live sessions). `lince/hello/1` never bumps — it lets a
  Cell one epoch behind report "needs updating" instead of going silent.
- Accept policy default-closed (`lince.discovery.accept_unknown = false`).
  `known` = `trust='known'` only. `blocked` closes both ALPNs before the ALPN
  split. `add_contact` defaults to `unknown`.
- Frames bounded (`MAX_FRAMES_PER_CONNECTION`); a batch/peer mismatch answers
  `Refused` (a security event, distinguishable from an ordinary failure).
- Discovery via `iroh-mdns-address-lookup` (pinned), Lince-specific mDNS
  service name, `organ_uid` no longer broadcast. `nearby` is a Protein
  source, not an Action + client poll; gated like `Decision`.
- Licensing: iroh MIT OR Apache-2.0, taken under MIT; ordinary crates.io dep.

### Peers and first contact

- Identity is a keypair, never an address. `GET /organ/nearby`, `POST
  /organ/pair` (dials `lince/thread/1`, binds reached NodeId).
- Introduction is a payload, not a challenge — connecting is the proof.
- Op-batch push carries `WireOp`/`OpBatch` only; facts ride hydrated inside
  their op. Import: hash-chain + signature check per fact; rejects land in
  `sync_quarantine`, rest of batch still applies. Idempotent by op identity
  AND fact uid.
- First-contact ranking: (1) QR in person — strongest, defeats relay
  interception; (2) paste into an already-trusted messaging app — equally
  strong; (3) discovery + conversation alone — weakest, acceptable only
  because (1)/(2) cover the real flows.
- **Pasted-contact reconciliation**: `organ_contact.pending_introduction`
  marks a code-added row; `Wire::reconcile_pending` dials `lince/thread/1`
  before every sync pass, adopts the peer's declared uid, refuses (leaves
  row pending) if the root key presented differs from the code's.
- The verification code (`verification_code`, base32 of a pubkey hash) is
  retired from all normal flows — iroh's address-is-the-key model leaves it
  defending only misdelivery, which QR/paste already defeat. Kept as an
  optional "verify this contact" panel for remote pairing.
- QR decode is backend (`engine::pairing::decode_qr`, rqrr), reached via the
  `media_capture` capability — chrome owns the camera, sand gets a string.
  **Known gap:** `getUserMedia` needs a secure context, so scanning fails
  over plain HTTP to a LAN hostname (surfaced explicitly, not as "no
  camera").

### The identity floor: root offline, operational keys online

- **Organ root key** signs only the Cell roster and key successions, and
  lives offline (hardware token / drawer). **Operational key** per Cell,
  used for everything routine — device compromise costs one revocable
  credential, never the identity.
- **The signed roster IS the certificate** — no separate object. Entry names
  a Cell, its operational key, its NodeId; roster carries monotonic version
  + expiry.
- Root key created **at most once ever** (no file + no roster = first boot;
  no file + a roster = root deliberately elsewhere). Detach verifies the
  copy byte-for-byte before deleting. Republishing preserves other members
  (dropping a name IS revocation).
- Revocations pulled before rosters every pass. Revocation = version bump +
  republish (monotonic, can't replay an old roster). Roster entries expire.
  Pre-signed revocation certificate written at key creation, lives with the
  root — one drawer trip both re-establishes identity and kills the old key.
- Enrolment: single-use, short-lived token over `lince/thread/1`
  (`lincecell1|` prefix, distinct from the contact `lince1|` prefix). Order
  is the security property: joining Cell mints its key and sends `Enrol`
  first, adopts the identity only once a roster comes back naming it,
  signed by the offered root. Refused enrolment leaves the device untouched.
  Enrolment window doubles as door policy (thread door open only while a
  token is live). Joining refused if the Cell already holds Records or has
  already published an identity. First boot's own (never-sent) ops are
  purged, not re-stamped.
- ALPNs versioned, roster versioned + expiring, iroh pinned to an exact
  version (like Loro), key-succession tables exist.

### Cell Record vs Organ Record

- Each Cell's DB holds two `kind=organ` Records: the **Cell Record** (this
  device, `cells::local()`, fixed slug `local-cell`, RAW insert — never
  syncs) and the **Organ Record** (the person, holds identity key + roster).
  Both minted by `Store::open`, never a later bootstrap step.
- `sync_op.actor_cell` is the op identity (`UNIQUE(actor_cell, hlc)`);
  `sync_op.organ_uid` is whose it is. **Cells must never share a uid** — two
  Cells minting the same HLC under one uid collide and import silently
  drops one as already-seen.
- `record.organ_uid` required, enforced by insert/update triggers.
- Operational keys are **per-Cell** (`ed25519:cell:<cell_uid>:v1`), the uid
  stays the Organ. `key_chains` (which keys may endorse a roster) is
  filtered to **root keys only** — an operational key must never validate a
  roster.
- No shared node key across devices: iroh's NodeId→address record would
  have two Cells overwrite each other, and it would force the Organ
  private key onto every device.
- Roster governs **admission live** (current roster, every connection);
  durable artifacts (envelopes, receipts) verify from **stored**
  `identity_key` rows — revocation is eventually consistent, stated in the UI
  ("your other devices apply it as they sync").

### Sibling sync (multiple Cells of one Organ)

- The signed roster IS the sibling list (`organ_contact` can't represent a
  sibling — it's keyed by the *other* Organ's uid).
- `CAP_WRITE` is the bar for accepting a sibling's ops, not membership — a
  relay Cell in the roster holds no capabilities.
- Siblings are **pull-only** (outbox is keyed by `contact_organ`, not Cell);
  no per-sibling cursor — the version vector is derived from our own log
  every pass.
- Roster expiry is **not** consulted between siblings (would deadlock: the
  only path to a fresher roster runs through a sibling).
- Pruning does not account for siblings (safe only because retention is
  superseded-only — a returning sibling loses nothing but intermediate LWW
  values).
- Verified across real OS processes (`cell_worker` host/join modes, two DBs,
  two independent HLC clocks).

### Threads (individual replica)

- A thread is not a subsystem — it is Records synced with exactly one peer.
  Sharing = granting sync access to a Record; that grant is **individual
  replica**, distinct from the whole-Organ `sync_out`/`sync_in` feed. Both
  gates run; neither substitutes for the other.
- Synced unit: **one Record per relationship** (`conversation`), with
  threads and messages nested inside via `replica_root` (denormalized,
  **local-only, immutable**, stamped at creation not grant time — no
  adoption of pre-existing Records into a root). One root per Record; a root
  may be granted to many contacts. Import enforces immutability (apply /
  quarantine / create-in-channel, three cases).
- Messaging is **not** collab: messages are ordinary Records, `set` ops,
  ordered by `record.created_hlc` (origin's stamp, never re-stamped on
  import — pinned by a clock-skew test). Two people opening the *same*
  message Record does get ordinary collab (Loro merge, cursors).
- Revocation is at the **conversation** level: reach closes when either
  party blocks the other, or deletes the shared Record. Deletion = local
  removal + explicit grant revocation; **no tombstone is emitted** (would
  delete the peer's copy too — their copy is theirs).
- Invites: `kind='thread_invite'`, one pending per Organ (`UNIQUE
  from_organ`), written with plain SQL (never `records::create`, which
  would push the offer to every contact). Grant row and invite are separate
  tables. Board surface: `/host/notifications`, 5s toast.
- Offline delivery already worked with no announce protocol — the outbox
  retry IS the delivery.
- No message-layer sealing for direct threads: iroh QUIC/TLS 1.3 already
  gives forward secrecy for the 1-hop case; sealing only earns its cost when
  a third party (the mailbox) carries the bytes.
- Individual-replica consent requires **both sides**: `OfferGrant` records
  `offered`, outbox fans out only to `accepted` grants, import refuses an
  unaccepted channel.
- Message Records structurally cannot ride the general feed (every
  general-feed query filters `replica_root IS NULL`).
- Key exchange promotion built: a `lince1|` code posted in a message renders
  an "Add as known" control; the local user always types the name
  themselves (never prefilled from the sender's claim).
- Conversation view (Record sand): thread tabs, composer, per-message
  delete, @-reference hops, inline images, thread search.

### Live references (mention without copy)

- Mentioning a Record in a thread **references it live**, never copies it —
  built 2026-08-14. `WireRequest::FetchReference` resolves through the §12
  gate on the thread door, in fixed order: grant on root → message actually
  references it → ordinary hide-list/scope gate. Withheld/retracted/never-
  mentioned/deleted-since all return the **same** refusal (no probing).
- No cache anywhere on this path — that's what makes revocation real
  (unlike everywhere else, where stopping a share is a request the remote
  may honour).
- Reading a reference is a read receipt to the owner (Organ-level count +
  last-read time, never a per-read row, never logging refusals).
- "Send a copy" is a separate, explicit, irreversible act — new uid, source
  untouched, permissioned as `record:create` not as an update.
- Delete-conversation emits no tombstone (their copy survives); read
  receipts inside a deleted conversation are deleted with it.

### Live mode

- `live` = in-memory session against a remote Organ (data never persisted
  locally); `replica` = local persistent copy. The mode is about *where the
  data lives*, not about connection pinning.
- Live sessions ride iroh on `lince/live/1`: a guest's browser opens an
  ordinary websocket to its **own** Cell on localhost, which relays frames
  to the host over iroh — no hostname, no cert, survives a network change.
- **One human reference: the Person.** `app_user`/`Person` merged
  (migration 0050) — the split cost real behaviour (a live guest could read
  but never write). `person_credential` is local-only, never synced.
- `visible_targets` includes the creator intrinsically (records with no
  actor at all stay invisible by design).
- The board can *be* the guest: `setLiveOrgan(uid)` repoints the single
  board socket at the relay; one value for the whole board (no mixed-source
  panels). Frames queued for our Cell are dropped on switch.
- A live guest's write lands on the host as the Person their login named —
  signed-envelope challenge, same path a browser walks.
- `organ_login` is a **binding, not a credential** — no password, the
  handshake already proved the Organ; grants a named Person's visibility.
  Requires `trust='known'`.
- Collab read gate: `may_read_record` gates `CollabJoin`/`CollabUpdate`.
  Presence (cursor + identity) gated the same way, and is ephemeral (lanes,
  never the op log).
- `lince --server` = `HttpServeMode::ApiOnly`: drops `/`, static assets,
  every board route; keeps auth, websocket, `/organ/*`. Forces local auth on
  (never persisted); refuses to start with no admin and no terminal to make
  one.

### Op log, reactive deltas, catch-up

- `sync_op(seq, tbl, uid, field, kind, value, hlc, actor_cell, organ_uid)`,
  `UNIQUE(actor_cell, hlc)` is both op identity and import idempotency key.
- Catch-up runs on a **version vector** (`FetchOpsSince`, replacing a raw
  seq cursor that broke under peer-side pruning). The vector names only the
  Cells of the Organ being asked (never our whole log — no third-party
  disclosure). Relay is off on both push and pull (`op.organ_uid ==
  from_organ` enforced on serve too, not just import). Grant-channel
  catch-up (conversations) got the same vectorization.
- Retention floor derived from what the peer's vector shows they actually
  hold, not what they claim.

### Merge and retention

- Loro doc per record for text only; scalars/extensions/assertions/concepts
  stay homebrew LWW. Movable lists rejected — kanban order is Protein's
  (exact, total, one-directional), nothing for a CRDT to reconcile.
- **Op retention** (`store::sync_ops::prune`): only **superseded** ops are
  prunable — a newer op exists for the same `(tbl, uid, field)`. This alone
  makes replica bootstrap ordinary catch-up: the log always retains the tip
  of every live field.
- `snapshot` is a first-class op kind (Loro snapshot, signed by the
  compacting Cell's own `(actor_cell, hlc)` — no synthesized identity
  needed). Supersede is kind-aware (a snapshot supersedes a record's crdt
  tail, never its tombstone). Snapshot ops are logged/served, never queued.
- Read-model rebuild + audit built: `audit_read_model` compares the
  materialized state against the log; `rebuild_read_model` replays in HLC
  order (last-write-wins = replay order, so no comparison machinery
  needed). Quantity is skipped on rebuild (it's additive, not assignable —
  `bump_quantity` adds on import, never assigns, fixed 2026-08-10).
- Cross-Organ audit (`FetchVector`, "Check we agree" button) reports
  disagreement without repairing — a peer legitimately prunes or hasn't
  drained yet, so silent re-send would hide a real non-convergence.
- Per-op read-compare-append-materialise is atomic in-process
  (`Engine::import_lock` over a whole batch). **Not** yet a cross-process
  database-level guard (needed once two Cells share a literal DB file).

### Op authenticity and inbound scope

- Attribution is the authenticated connection: `op.organ_uid ==
  batch.from_organ`, checked in `Engine::inadmissible`; no per-op signature
  needed because relaying defaults **off** (`sync_ops::append` never
  re-enqueues an imported op).
- `actor_cell` must be in the sender's roster (closes a pre-insert
  collision attack on the dedup key). The no-roster case is handled by the
  negative form — refuse an op naming a Cell some *other* Organ's roster
  claims, since refusing every rosterless sender would drop legitimate
  first-contact traffic.
- Clock: `MAX_CLOCK_DRIFT_MS` (5 min) bounds only future stamps; import
  quarantines rather than clamps. `next()` saturates.
- **Inbound Protein scope** (`organ_contact.accept_fields`) built, mirroring
  the outbound scope — same predicate (`op_in_scope`), same encoding, so
  remote writes are gated by permission too, not accept-everything.
  Out-of-scope ops are dropped silently (our policy working), never
  quarantined.

### Quarantine

- Bounded per-contact ring (`QUARANTINE_PER_CONTACT` = 200, oldest
  dropped) — one flooding contact can't evict another's evidence. Surfaced
  in the Organ sand per-contact, three states (not loaded / nothing refused
  / list), payload rendered as text never markup.

### Public directory (pkarr) and discovery UI

- `engine::directory` publishes the Organ's front door under its identity
  key via pkarr; `Wire::dial` falls back to it once every known Cell has
  failed. Signed once by the packet's own addressing keypair (the root) —
  no second signature layer.
- **Two-tier publishing**, forced by the DHT's ~1000-byte cap (a full
  roster doesn't fit): public tier = front-door Cell only; contact tier =
  full roster, shared over an authenticated connection.
- `Reach` is three states, defaults to **relay-only**: `Internet` (direct
  addresses + holepunch), `Relay` (relays only, no direct path published),
  `Local` (nothing published). mDNS is a separate switch, default **off**,
  with an expiry (`local_until`) — presence stops on its own.
- Front-door mechanics: a stranger's `Introduce` without `CAP_REPRESENT` is
  held in `door_request` (local-only, never in the op log) and fetched by a
  capable sibling. Fetch/release are separate verbs so a dying Cell doesn't
  drop the queue.
- Dialing is a race: every known candidate (saved NodeId + every roster
  Cell) starts together, staggered 150ms, LAN-local preferred; directory
  lookup is a second round only after every known candidate has lost.
- Discovery UI: nearby list shows a NodeId fingerprint beside the untrusted
  claimed name; Discovery panel states the cost of each toggle rather than
  presenting them as neutral.

### Person deactivation

- `lince.person.standing` flag, never a delete — history stays attributable,
  only the ability to act ends. **Absence means active** (fails toward
  running, matching the executor default).
- Travels between the Organ's own Cells only, dropped outbound to every
  contact, refused at the admissibility gate inbound.
- Enforced at all **five** doors: password, JWT, board websocket (re-read
  per frame, not just at upgrade), iroh live login, granted Organ login.
- Self-deactivation refused; the last active admin is protected
  (`user:update` alone can't close every admin). Refusal message is
  word-for-word identical to a wrong password (no account enumeration).

### Root succession

- `POST /organ/identity/succession` signs old-root-endorses-new-root;
  contacts pull via `FetchSuccessions`, adopt only if it chains from a key
  already held. Revocations processed before successions before roster,
  every pass.
- `key_chains` walks forward transitively — a contact offline across two
  rotations still validates. A revoked key cannot endorse a successor
  (`adopt_succession` calls `key_chains` on the *old* key).

### Multi-Cell recurring work

- The axis that matters: **local** consequences (recompute a view) are safe
  to run on every Cell; **externally observable** ones (`Act` route in
  Karma) must happen exactly once.
- Karma: two independent axes, both built. Is the Rule **synced** —
  `lince.karma` Record extension, one op per definition, hash re-verified
  on arrival, only reachable to our own Cells (not contacts, not the
  outbound scope filter, which would otherwise leak rule text). Does
  **this Cell execute** it — `karma_program_execution`, local-only,
  never syncs, absence means execute.
- Designated executor (`lince.schedule.executor`, a synced Record
  extension, LWW) used **only** for transfer delivery retries (the one case
  that reaches outward with a real duplicate cost). Pruning and Organ
  polling deliberately do **not** take a designation — pruning is per-Cell
  by nature, polling duplicates are cheap and dedupe on arrival.
- No heartbeat/lease-with-expiry: in a mostly-offline-laptop topology it
  would fail over constantly in the steady state. Designation is a value
  (LWW), not a lock; local per-Cell flag and remote designation are both
  vetoes, ANDed, neither compels.
- Wire epochs: every protocol change queues against one numbered epoch per
  release train rather than shipping opportunistically, so the hard ALPN
  cut happens on a schedule.

### Mailbox (store-and-forward through a third party)

Built end to end, with ordinary crypto (ChaCha20-Poly1305 + X25519), **not**
Automerge/Keyhive (pre-alpha, no audit, would replace the whole CRDT stack).

- Seals a **batch**, not individual ops (per-op sealing would leak activity
  shape via count/timing).
- Sealing key is separate from the identity key and **rotated**, per-Cell
  (never shared across an Organ's devices), published inside the signed
  roster with its own `not_after`. Rotation + mailbox retention share one
  number (nothing uncollected is lost to a rotation; nothing past the
  window is ever openable again).
- Bundle authenticates its sender via an ed25519 signature by the sending
  Cell's operational key (an ephemeral seal alone proves nothing); the
  receiving side re-derives the sender from the signed roster, never trusts
  the payload's own claim.
- Mailbox is additive (`services.lince.mailbox.enable` alongside `mode`),
  not a mode — carrying is a favour, not a role.
- Own ALPN (`lince/mailbox/1`), open to anyone, serving exactly four verbs
  and no sync verbs at all — structurally, a carrier converges nothing.
- Registration stores the recipient's **root key**, not addresses — a
  collector presents its current roster, chained from the registered root,
  so device changes need no re-registration. One wording for every
  collection failure (no probing who a carrier serves).
- Expiry (30 days) deletes ciphertext but keeps a routing notice so the
  sender can be told later.
- Asking to carry (`lincemail1|` invite code, 7-day TTL) and declining are
  both built; a decline tells the asker nothing (keeps saying no cheap).
  Nothing on the wire can commit the operator — `MailboxAsked` only, never
  yes/no.
- Sender is told when mail expired uncollected, via a **pull**
  (`MailboxExpiries`, scoped by the QUIC node identity the carrier actually
  saw, never a claimed Organ/Cell string).
- Retry window: a contact is mailed only after 10 minutes of silence
  (`Wire::MAIL_AFTER`), not on first failed dial — most failures are
  transient. Mailing doesn't move `peer_acked_seq` (a bundle on a stranger's
  disk isn't confirmed receipt).
- Pickup points published inside the signed roster (`Roster.pickup`);
  publishing probes the carrier first and only a `Refused` blocks it
  (silence ≠ refusal, most machines are asleep most of the time).

### Infrastructure (NixOS)

- Front door (`mode = "front-door"`, renamed from `"relay"` 2026-08-15) is
  a Cell in the roster with **no capabilities**, enforced by a database
  trigger below every client — not merely a convention.
- `iroh-relay` and a Lince Cell can coexist on one VPS as separate
  processes/users/ports; `vps-coexistence` Nix check refuses four specific
  blurrings (shared user, shared group, port collision, nested state dir).
- Per-peer connection cap (`MAX_CONNECTIONS_PER_PEER` = 8) ships from the
  first deploy. Operator-facing statements about what a relay/front-door
  can and cannot see/do live in the product UI, not a README.
- Discovery settings (`local`/`internet`/`direct`) live on the **Cell**
  Record (raw writes, never synced) — they describe a machine, not an
  identity. `wire_supervisor` rebinds the endpoint live on change.

---

## Decided against

Kept short so nobody re-proposes them.

- **At-rest encryption of record bodies/op values** — dropped 2026-08-03.
  Every reader would need to decrypt (query, Protein, search, export, File
  Sync) and a missed one fails silently; it defends only a stolen disk/
  backup, which full-disk encryption already covers for free.
- **Time-locked key succession** — cut. Requires clock agreement and an
  online victim during the veto window; the pre-signed revocation
  certificate handles the same case immediately with no clock assumptions.
- **M-of-N social recovery** — rejected. Every recovery path is also an
  attack path; root-offline + succession chains already make the
  catastrophic case rare.
- **One shared node key across devices** — rejected. iroh's NodeId→address
  publishing means two endpoints overwrite each other; also forces the
  identity key onto every device.
- **A dual `url | node_id` address kind** — rejected; local dev DBs are
  expendable, a contact is reached by NodeId only.
- **A Loro map for scalar/extension fields** — rejected 2026-08-07. Those
  are already per-key LWW with their own HLCs; a second CRDT authority over
  one value can only silently disagree with the first.
- **A bootstrap/snapshot wire protocol** — not needed once retention became
  superseded-only; replaying from zero already is the bootstrap.
- **Movable lists as a CRDT** — not needed; kanban order is Protein's, not
  a per-card position two people can concurrently drag.
- **Sync/transfer merge (full)** — rejected 2026-08-09; opposite delivery
  guarantees (sync is idempotent/endless, transfer is exactly-once with an
  ack). Only the consent handshake, retry scheduler, and delivery-status
  surface were unified; delivery semantics and domain objects stay apart.
- **A public Lince mailbox shipped as a default seed** — rejected;
  centralises timing metadata onto one well-known operator. Registration-
  by-ask replaces any routing/load-balancing idea.
- **Identity verification via `lince.txt`-on-a-website** — dropped; proves
  domain control, not that the announcing Organ holds the key. A profile is
  never evidence.
- **A full Mainline DHT node run by Lince** — do not run one; marginal
  contribution is a rounding error against millions of existing nodes, cost
  is a scraped, amplification-prone public UDP service.

---

## What is left

### Identity / roster
- [ ] Key recovery (lost signing key) — deliberately separate from Person
  deactivation; not started.
- [ ] Absorbing what a dormant Person leaves behind (open Needs, custody,
  unfinished Transfers) — must never be automatic.
- [ ] The local surface config (`baseUrl`, `aliases`, `local`) still lives
  on the syncing Organ Record; belongs on the Cell Record.
- [ ] `RosterStatus`/succession UI for the stored `identity_succession` /
  `record_succession` chain — data exists, no surface yet.

### Transport / discovery
- [ ] Ordinary HTTPS login path (hostname, cert, reverse proxy) — no longer
  on the critical path (live-over-iroh replaced it) but still the only way
  a plain browser reaches a Cell that isn't its own.
- [ ] Off-LAN camera scanning — `getUserMedia` needs a secure context;
  blocked on the hostname/TLS item above.
- [ ] Live sessions do not resume after a real connection loss (only
  network-path migration is handled); needs a session id + replay policy.
- [ ] `contact.mode` (replica vs live) column is unused — wire it or drop it.
- [ ] Per-contact rate limiting against an empty-vector "send me everything"
  catch-up abuse (filed under Scoping).
- [ ] Third of the two-part dialing preference — "prefer the always-on Cell
  for bulk" is not implemented (only LAN-local preference is).

### Infrastructure (blocked on a machine, not code)
- [ ] Running `iroh-relay` on the VPS as a deployed instance.
- [ ] A Cell on the VPS as a live roster member (always-on device).
- [ ] The relay-only-costs-nothing conclusion, observed rather than argued —
  moved to Resenha as a simulated-network scenario (needs a second machine
  to be true rather than assumed).
- [ ] `Engine::cell_may` capability check (write/karma/represent) is defined
  and signed into the roster but **nothing calls it yet** — no decision
  point consumes it.
- [ ] Facade storage quota under front-door config — placeholder only, no
  Facade generator exists yet.

### Threads / replica
- [ ] Replica bootstrap for a *new* contact receiving history that predates
  the connection (today: ops flow from the moment you connect, nothing
  earlier). Needs: snapshot vs replay-the-log, paging, honest partial-copy
  presentation.
- [ ] Collapse `create-message` vs `send-message` into one Action (both
  correct, just duplicated).
- [ ] Unify the four repeated consent-handshake implementations (thread
  invites, replica agreement, tree acceptance, transfer acceptance) into one.

### Collab
- [ ] Relations sand has no text embed at all (its record sidepanels were
  removed by an earlier decision) — reversing that is a design call.
- [ ] Kanban/table embeds degrade silently to a plain input when the shared
  doc is unreachable — no liveness-lost indicator.
- [ ] Only the record editor renders cursors; other embeds sync text with no
  presence.
- [ ] Collab wasm loading inside a sand **iframe** is unproven by any test —
  only real-Node execution is covered; needs a browser test.
- [ ] Storage: a compacted doc's snapshot is stored twice (BLOB in
  `record_doc` + base64 on the op) — deliberate, but collapsing to one copy
  trades disk for read-path latency if ever revisited.
- [ ] Storage budget ceilings beyond decision 2's snapshot/truncate (two of
  three consumers don't exist yet).

### Multi-Cell / Karma
- [ ] The default for a newly-activated *externally observable* Karma rule's
  designated executor is unset — no Cell is marked "the always-on one" yet,
  so the obvious "designate the activating Cell" default is known-wrong and
  nothing has replaced it.
- [ ] Cross-process database-level guard for the atomic import
  read-compare-append sequence (only the in-process lock exists; needed once
  two Cells literally share one DB file).
- [ ] Multi-Cell scenarios that need Resenha (untestable from one process):
  designated-executor exactly-once across three Cells; an unreachable holder
  never handing off the lease; two Cells not double-retrying one transfer
  delivery.
- [ ] Karma cycle fuel/ceiling for *dispatched* rule-produced ops — not
  buildable yet because nothing dispatches an accepted candidate into an op
  (Karma intents are still inert).

### Audit / retention
- [ ] Nothing recomputes the read model from the log on a schedule — a
  divergence found by `audit_read_model` doesn't self-heal without someone
  running `audit_and_repair`.
- [ ] A contradiction in this document to resolve: at-rest encryption was
  formally dropped, but older prose elsewhere reasoned as if it still
  existed — every remaining reference should say "encrypted in transit,
  forward secret, plaintext at rest."

### Multi-hop discovery (not started)
- [ ] Gossip cache / delegated ask-around search with hop/TTL limits, for
  "there is a Need three hops away I've never heard of." Only the hop-limit
  reasoning is written down; no mechanism exists.
- [ ] World-reach discovery beyond gossip — named as a distinct mechanism,
  not designed.

### Proximity (parked, not technical — owner decision 2026-08-09)
- [ ] Same-network / BLE / UWB physical-nearness scoring
  (`organ_contact.proximity`) — deliberately not built. If revisited: local-
  only, never travels, only ever for existing contacts (never introduces
  strangers), bucketed (immediate/near/far) never metres.
}



## Visibility: what a logged-in Organ may see

When someone logs into your Organ and can read your records, they see
everything by default. You choose to hide things instead of choosing to
share them.

### Done

- **Hide a whole record from a contact.** In the contact panel, there is a
  "Never send" field where you list records that a specific contact should
  never receive. If a hidden record is linked to another record, that link
  is hidden too, so a hidden record's connections can't be seen either.
  Hiding only affects what's sent from now on — it doesn't take back
  anything they already have, and un-hiding doesn't resend anything they
  missed while it was hidden.

- **Choose which parts of a record a contact sees, not just yes/no.** A
  record can have several parts (e.g. its title vs. its full content). For
  each contact you can choose one of three settings: share everything, share
  only the parts you pick, or share nothing but the fact that the record
  exists. Some things always travel as a whole (you can't split a shared
  document into "just the title"); the choice tells you clearly when a
  setting isn't possible.

- **Deleted records are always reported as deleted**, even to a narrowed
  contact — otherwise their copy would never know the record was removed
  and would live on forever on their side.

- **Widening what a contact sees catches them up automatically.** If you add
  a part to what a contact can see, they get everything they missed for that
  part, not just new changes going forward. Narrowing never does this in
  reverse — it only stops future sharing, it never retracts what they
  already received.

- **If your sharing settings for a contact can't be read** (corrupted or
  broken), Lince treats that contact as fully unnarrowed for safety, and
  tells you clearly in the panel that the setting isn't being applied and
  shows you the broken value, so you can fix it by re-saving.

- **When you unhide a contact, they get caught up properly**, including
  anything that changed for that record while it was hidden — not just
  future changes.

- **The same "which parts of a record" picker is used everywhere**, not
  just here. It's the same one already used elsewhere to choose what a
  shared record includes, so learning it in one place carries over to the
  other.

### What is left

- **A summary view of what each contact can see of you.** Today you can set
  each contact's sharing level one at a time in that contact's own panel,
  but there's no single screen listing all contacts side by side with what
  each one currently sees. *Technical: `organ_contact.scope_fields` is set
  and read per-contact already; this is a read-only aggregate view over the
  existing `organ_contact` rows, no new storage or sync change.*

- **Per-device sharing limits.** Right now a device you've logged in from
  (a phone, another computer) either has full access to your Organ or is
  logged out entirely — there's no in-between where you let a device see
  less than everything. *Technical: device/session membership today is
  tracked as an all-or-nothing entry in the roster (`identity_key` /
  revocation), with no `scope_fields`-style column-narrowing on it; adding
  this means giving devices the same kind of scope the per-contact sharing
  narrowing already has, applied to roster membership instead of contacts.*
